From: claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 10:44:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: partnerships page, privacy policy, and a logo that
 reaches home

The logo in the header and footer already pointed at "/", but "/" redirected
anyone signed in to /dashboard, so the homepage was unreachable for everybody
with an account. "/" is now the homepage for everyone; landing on the dashboard
after signing in is Clerk's post-sign-in destination instead, which is where
that behaviour belongs.

Partnerships is a real page with a working form. The enquiry is delivered by
email and stored nowhere, which sets the standard the route has to meet: a
failed send is never reported as success, the page prints a direct address to
fall back on, and a failure writes the full enquiry to the log because it is
the only remaining copy.

This is the first endpoint on the server that writes without a signed-in user
behind it, so it is guarded three ways: a hidden field the crude scrapers fill
and people never see, a small per-address hourly budget, and validation that
refuses anything unusable before a mail provider is contacted. The budget is
spent only on submissions that were otherwise valid, so somebody fumbling their
email address five times is not locked out before they ever send anything.

Express now trusts one proxy hop. Without it every request appears to come from
Railway's proxy, the whole internet shares one apparent address, and the second
genuine partner of the day is turned away. One hop rather than `true`, so a
caller cannot prepend an X-Forwarded-For and mint a fresh identity per request.

The privacy policy is adapted from the Africa Energy Pulse one rather than
copied. That policy covers a publication with a newsletter; this is a platform
holding accounts, attendance, submitted coursework and peer critique, and
claiming to collect less than we do would be worse than having no policy. It
names the processors that actually see the data, including that class material
goes to an AI provider when a facilitator uses the drafting tool.

Terms of Service is gone rather than left as a dead "#" link.

